 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Empty Space:** Use a binary occupancy grid (e.g., via NerfAcc) to skip samples in empty areas [S1].
-*   **Precision:** The derivation of Ripmap levels from the base level ensures consistency but requires careful implementation of average pooling to avoid artifacts [S1].
-*   **Coordinate Mapping:** The exact mapping from 2D Gaussian parameters to Ripmap grid coordinates and level indices is not fully detailed in the source [G].
+*   **Empty Space:** Use binary occupancy grid (via NerfAcc) to skip samples in empty areas, improving efficiency [S1].
+*   **Level Calculation:** The levels $l_x, l_y$ must be non-negative. Clamping is necessary if the Gaussian footprint is very small [G].
+*   **Projection Matrices:** Must be correctly derived for the chosen Platonic solid (e.g., icosahedron) to ensure accurate 2D projection of the 3D Gaussian [S1].
+
+## Subtopics
+
+### Ripmap Encoding
+A hierarchical feature grid structure that uses average pooling to derive lower levels from a learnable base level, enabling efficient anisotropic area sampling [S1].
+
+### Platonic Solid Projection
+A technique to project 3D Gaussians onto the faces of a Platonic solid (e.g., icosahedron) to form 2D Gaussians, which are then sampled using Ripmap Encoding [S1].
+
+### Area Loss ($L_{area}$)
+A loss scaling factor that incorporates the area of the pixel footprint on the image plane to make the photometric loss area-aware, improving anti-aliasing [S1].
 
